      "outputTokens": 3604,
      "costUsd": 0.032686,
      "pronunciationText": "Here are the two investments from the end of the last video, one in each jar. Ten thousand dollars at six per cent a year, for ten years. The only difference is one phrase. Simple interest pays six per cent of the original amount every year, so the account grows by the same six hundred dollars each time. That's an arithmetic model, and after ten years it's sixteen thousand dollars. Compound interest pays six per cent of the current balance, so each year multiplies it by one point zero six. That's a geometric model, and after ten years it's seventeen thousand, nine hundred and eight dollars, forty-eight. Same numbers, different words, different model. So which words justify which model?"
    },
