      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 510,
      "outputTokens": 3698,
      "costUsd": 0.033537,
      "pronunciationText": "Probability questions work the same way. If the question lists the values with their probabilities, it's a discrete distribution, and the probabilities add to one. If it gives a density function, probabilities are areas under it, and the total area is one. If equal-width parts of an interval are equally likely, the density is flat, one over the width of the interval. And if it says normally distributed, with a mean and a standard deviation, the curve is a bell, and the empirical rule or z-scores give the probabilities. Here's a test. A question asks how likely a delivery is to take more than fifty-eight minutes. Which model fits? Think for three seconds. You can't tell yet. The question has to say which shape it means."
    },
