      "successfulRequestCount": 0,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 0,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 0,
      "outputTokens": 0,
      "costUsd": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "First, suppose the time is uniform from forty to sixty minutes, so equal stretches of time are equally likely. The density is flat, at one over twenty. More than fifty-eight minutes is the last two minutes of the twenty, so the probability is two twentieths, which is one in ten. Now suppose instead the times are normally distributed, with mean fifty and standard deviation four. Fifty-eight is two standard deviations above the mean. About ninety-five per cent of times are within two standard deviations, so about five per cent are outside, and half of that is above. That's about two point five per cent. Same threshold, different shape, so a different answer. And the normal model isn't even limited to forty to sixty. The normal curve piles its probability near the mean, and leaves only ay thin tail out there."
    },
